\section{Why the bootstrap target is the \emph{fixed-subgroup} effect}{

A naive bootstrap of \code{dina_subgroup()}'s \code{mean_tau_hat} is
uninformative.  The search rule selects the \emph{largest} subgroup
whose mean tau-hat exceeds \code{m_diff}, so the chosen subgroup lies
at the boundary by construction: adding one more patient would
push the mean below \code{m_diff}.  Each bootstrap iteration's
selected subgroup is similarly pinned, so the bootstrap
distribution of \code{mean_tau_hat} clusters tightly above \code{m_diff}
and the resulting CI quantifies threshold-grid granularity
rather than effect uncertainty.  The fixed-subgroup linear
functional \verb{a*^T beta_b} instead targets the clinically
meaningful estimand "treatment effect for patients whose
covariates lie in the discovered subgroup".  Because DINA's
\code{beta-hat} is cross-fit (and hence asymptotically unbiased even
for data-driven contrasts), the percentile distribution of
\verb{a*^T beta_b} gives a non-parametric CI that complements the
sandwich Wald CI of \code{dina_subgroup()}.
}

\section{Performance}{

With \code{parallel = "boots"}, each bootstrap iteration runs in a worker
via \verb{foreach::foreach() \%dofuture\%}.  Set \code{future::plan()} to a
parallel backend (e.g., \code{future::multisession}) before calling, and
reset to \code{"sequential"} afterwards.  Workers run from the installed
package; \code{devtools::install()} is required after code edits before
the parallel branch will see them.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
set.seed(1)
n <- 400
df_demo <- data.frame(
  w  = stats::rbinom(n, 1, 0.5),
  x1 = stats::runif(n, -1, 1),
  x2 = stats::runif(n, -1, 1)
)
tau_x      <- 0.4 + 1.2 * df_demo$x1
df_demo$y  <- 0.5 * df_demo$x1 + df_demo$w * tau_x + stats::rnorm(n)

bs <- dina_subgroup_bootstrap(
  df         = df_demo,
  outcome    = "y",
  treatment  = "w",
  covariates = c("x1", "x2"),
  family     = "gaussian",
  m_diff     = 0.5,
  n_boot     = 50L,
  seed       = 1L
)
print(bs)
}

